/*--------------------------------*- C++ -*----------------------------------*\
  VMFL003 -- turbulent viscosity.  ALGEBRAIC (nut = Cmu k^2 / epsilon), not
  solved, so it has no entry in fvSolution.

  THE WALL TREATMENT IS THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL CHOICE IN THIS CASE AND IT IS
  DECLARED, NOT DEFAULTED.  `nutkWallFunction` computes the first-cell
  turbulent viscosity from the LOG LAW using a friction velocity built from k
  (u* = Cmu^0.25 sqrt(k)), which is the direct OpenFOAM analogue of Fluent's
  and CFX's STANDARD wall function -- the treatment the manual names ("standard
  k-epsilon Model", p. 19).  A continuous / all-y+ alternative
  (nutUSpaldingWallFunction) was considered and DECLINED: it is a different
  wall treatment from the one the manual ran, and this case's whole purpose is
  to measure the lab's reproduction of the manual's stated model.

  nutkWallFunction is valid only where the first cell centre lies in the log
  layer.  That is why the radial mesh is FIXED at NR = 5 across the entire
  Roache triple (predicted first-cell y+ = 40.835033970764385, mid-log-layer)
  and why the comparator carries a one-way NOT-A-RESULT clause on the measured
  mean y+ outside [25, 65].  PREREGISTRATION.md section 4.2 derives why a
  ratio-2 refinement in the RADIAL direction is IMPOSSIBLE for this case
  without leaving the log layer: the pipe radius is only
  R+ = R u_tau / nu = 408.3503397076439 wall units.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       volScalarField;
    object      nut;
}
// * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * //

dimensions      [0 2 -1 0 0 0 0];

internalField   uniform 0;

boundaryField
{
    inlet
    {
        type            calculated;
        value           uniform 0;
    }

    outlet
    {
        type            calculated;
        value           uniform 0;
    }

    walls
    {
        type            nutkWallFunction;
        value           uniform 0;
    }

    wedge1
    {
        type            wedge;
    }

    wedge2
    {
        type            wedge;
    }
}

// ************************************************************************* //
